      "created_at": "2017-03-05T00:27:34Z",
      "updated_at": "2017-03-05T00:35:20Z",
      "body": "From the above, the best solution seems to be to just rewrite the documentation markup a little bit, to use an anchor with a name instead of another element with an \"id\", since ad-blockers target \"id\" but not \"name\":\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<h1><a name=\"cookies\">Cookies</a></h1>\r\n<div id=\"cookies-section\">text here...</a>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThat was confirmed working. You can jump to such named anchors via `#cookies` in the URL bar. And since there's no \"src\" on the anchor, it won't cause the browser to draw it as a hyperlink with underlines. It's also possible to just do `<h1><a name=\"cookies\"></a>Cookies</h1>` but I heard that's invalid XHTML (which is what you serve the docs as).",
      "author_association": "NONE",
      "pin": null,
      "reactions": {
